pread to distant body sites. This relationship highlights that the community’s scope includes not only localized disease but also advanced dissemination categories that affect prognosis and treatment planning. [Data: Entities (3415); Relationships (6809)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Symptoms and symptom overlap create potential for diagnostic ambiguity, especially versus BPH\",\"explanation\":\"Symptoms are a dedicated concept linked to prostate cancer, indicating that symptom discussion is an expected part of the prostate cancer overview and evaluation process. Specific possible symptoms in the network include blood in urine or semen and back pain, both directly linked as potential manifestations. [Data: Entities (3148, 3165, 3167); Relationships (6142, 6221, 6226)]\\n\\nThe network also emphasizes that benign prostatic hyperplasia (BPH) can have overlapping symptoms with prostate cancer, and that symptoms alone may be difficult to distinguish—implying the need for further evaluation rather than symptom-based conclusions. This overlap is a key clinical dynamic in the community: a noncancerous condition can mimic cancer presentation. [Data: Entities (3394); Relationships (6728)]\"},{\"summary\":\"PSA is framed as a bloodstream-measured indicator that may signal prostate cancer\",\"explanation\":\"PSA (prostate-specific antigen) is represented as a measurable substance whose unusually high blood level can be a possible sign of prostate cancer. The relationships explicitly situate PSA measurement in the bloodstream, reinforcing PSA’s role as a testable biomarker rather than a symptom. [Data: Entities (3320); Relationships (6576)]\\n\\nThe entity descriptions further connect PSA production to prostate cells, including normal prostate cells, clarifying why PSA can appear in blood and vary with prostate-related conditions. This provides biological context for why PSA is used in screening and evaluation discussions. [Data: Entities (3158); Relationships (6417)]\"},{\"summary\":\"Ra